hat disrupted.\n\nAlso note: T1 was just discarded by me earlier? No \u2014 history says I played J1, then Chi T2, then Play W9. Wait \u2014 contradiction?\n\nWait: my hand now includes T1, but I supposedly \"Chi T2\" \u2014 that means I formed a Chow using T2 discarded by left player, using my T1 and T3? But I don\u2019t have T3 now...\n\nReview game history:\n- My history: Play J1, Chi T2, Play W9\n- After Chi T2, I must have added T2 to my hand to form [T1,T2,T3] meld \u2014 meaning I had T1 and T3 before Chi.\nBut now my hand shows T1:1, T3 not present \u2192 consistent, since T3 was used in meld.\n\nSo yes, I used T1 and T3 from hand + claimed T2 to form [T1,T2,T3].\n\nNow, current hand: [W3\u00d72, W5, T1, T4, T7, T8, B7\u00d72, F1, J3]\n\nSo T1 is still here? That suggests I drew it back? Or typo?\n\nNo \u2014 after forming a meld via Chi, you place the three tiles face-up. So once [T1,T2,T3] is melded, those tiles are no longer in hand.\n\nBut the state says: \"Your hand tiles are: [W3: 2; W5: 1; T1: 1; T4: 1; T7: 1; T8: 1; B7: 2; F1: 1; J3: 1]\" \u2014 implies T1 is *still* in hand.\n\nThis is impossible unless I later drew another T1.\n\nPossibility: the system allows having a tile both in hand and in meld? No \u2014 standard rules: melded tiles are removed from hand.\n\nThus, most plausible explanation: **the T1 listed in hand is a mistake**, or it refers to a *different* T1 drawn afterward? But there are only four T1s.\n\nCheck unshown: T1:2 \u2014 meaning 2 remain unseen. Since one was used in my Chi, and assuming none discarded yet beyond that, possible that one T1 remains in wall and one in play.\n\nBut if I used T1 in meld, I shouldn't have another unless I drew it.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps after forming [T1,T2,T3], I kept playing, and later drew another T1? Possible.\n\nGiven that T1 is listed in hand with count 1, we must assume it's true \u2014 I currently hold a T1 despite having used one in meld.\n\nThat means: total T1s:\n- 4 total\n- One in my meld \u2192 accounted\n- One in my hand now \u2192 second\n- Two unshown \u2192 possible\n\nYes, acceptable.\n\nStill, holding T1, T4, T7, T8 \u2014 all scattered.\n\nBack to evaluation:\n\nSeven Pairs requires seven distinct pairs. Currently, I have:\n- W3\u00d72 \u2192 1 pair\n- B7\u00d72 \u2192 1 pair\nOthers: W5, T1, T4, T7, T8, F1, J3 \u2014 all singles\n\nIf I can draw duplicates of any of these, possible.\n\nBut odds low. Alternative: go for All Chows (2 points) + possible combinations.\n\nBut better: look at **Half Flush** possibility \u2014 mostly W and honor? I have W3, W5, and some honors.\n\nHonors: F1 (East), J3 (White). Others are scattered.\n\nNot strong.\n\nAnother idea: **All Types** (8 points): need all three suits, winds, dragons.\n\nI have:\n- Characters (W): yes\n- Bamboos (T): yes\n- Dots (B): yes\n- Winds (F1): yes\n- Dragons (J3): yes\n\n\u2192 Already qualifies for **All Types (8 points)** if I win with current composition (but need full hand).\n\nPlus:\n- I have two pairs (W3, B7)\n- Could aim for Chicken Hand (8 points) \u2014 minimal but sufficient.\n\nChicken Hand is worth 8 points alone \u2014 enough to win.\n\nSo even a basic hand with no high-scoring patterns but totaling \u22658 points works.\n\nCurrent possible patterns:\n- All Chows: I have one melded Chow, and hand contains T1,T4,T7,T8 \u2014 could form chows if developed. But T1 is isolated unless get T2/T3 (but T2,T3 heavily used).\n- T7,T8 \u2192 need T6 or T9. T9 unshown:3, T6:3 \u2014 possible.\n- W3,W5 \u2192 gap at W4 (unshown:4) \u2014 possible W4.\n\nBut too scattered.\n\nBetter: minimize loss, aim for safe discards, avoid feeding opponents.\n\nOpponents:\n- Next player: has F2, J2 pungs \u2014 clearly going for All Honors or Big/Little Three Dragons.\n- Across: has B6-B7-B8, T2-T3-T4 \u2014 going for Full Flush or Pure Straight.\n- Previous: unknown.\n\nSo avoid discarding F1, J3 \u2014 both dangerous (dragons and winds). F1 is East (prevailing), J3 is White Dragon \u2014 both valuable.\n\nTherefore, **do NOT play F1 or J3** \u2014 high risk of being punged.\n\nSimilarly, B7: across has B6,B7,B8 \u2014 already completed meld, so B7 might be safe? But they may have kong? Unlikely \u2014 they\u2019d have declared Gang.\n\nStill, B7 is part of their meld \u2014 probably safe to discard one, but I have two \u2014 better keep one for pair.\n\nW3: no evidence others want.\n\nTiles to consider discarding: T1, T4, T7, T8, W5\n\nT4: across has [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 so they just completed that meld \u2014 very dangerous to discard T4 \u2014 they may want to kong!\n\nYes! They just Chi'd T3, then Play B3, Play T9 \u2014 likely finished building.\n\nBut exposing [T2,T3,T4] means they have one T4 \u2014 discard T4 now risks them declaring Kong.\n\n**Do NOT play T4**\n\nT1: I have one, unshown:2 \u2014 not obviously dangerous, but left player (across) played B3, W8 \u2014 not clear.\n\nBut T1 was used in my own Chi \u2014 others may have interest?\n\nNext player history: no T1 discard.\n\nPrevious player: no T1.\n\nAcross: no.\n\nBut I used T1 \u2014 so one gone.\n\nStill, T1 relatively safe.\n\nT7, T8: across has no high bamboo except early ones. T7,T8 not seen in their melds.\n\nUnshown T7:4 \u2192 all still available? No: total 4, none shown \u2192 yes, all unshown.\n\nWait: unshown T7:4 \u2192 means none discarded or exposed \u2192 safe?\n\nBut I have T7 and T8 \u2014 could form T7-T8-T9 if draw T9.\n\nT9 unshown:3 \u2014 possible.\n\nAlternatively, discard **W5** \u2014 it's isolated, W3 and W5 not easily forming chow without W4.\n\nAnd W4 unshown:4 \u2014 possible, but not guaranteed.\n\nBetween T7, T8, W5 \u2014 which is least useful?\n\nT7 and T8 together offer potential for Chow.\n\nW5 alone \u2014 unless I draw W4/W6, useless.\n\nSo **W5** is weakest.\n\nAlso, W3\u00d72 + W5 \u2014 cannot form pung.\n\nWhereas T7+T8 can become part of Chow.\n\nSo best discard: **W5**\n\nOther option: **T1** \u2014 singleton, no support.\n\nBut T1 is terminal, could be useful for edge waits, but low priority.\n\nFinal decision: **Play W5** \u2014 it\u2019s a middle tile, not part of any strong sequence, and keeping it blocks pair development.\n\nReason: W5 is an isolated tile with little potential to contribute to melds or pairs, while discarding it avoids feeding opponents (especially compared to T4, F1, J3, etc.). It minimizes risk and helps streamline toward a potential Chicken Hand or Seven Pairs strategy.\n\nAnswer: Play W5"}
