 (6 points) if we can convert chows into pungs. But we already have a Chow \u2014 hard to shift.\n- Best scoring hope: **Mixed Straight** (8 points): need 123, 456, 789 in different suits. We already have T1,T2,T3 \u2192 123 in Bamboo. Need 456 and 789. T5, T7, T8 could help build toward that.\n    - T5 is middle of 456, T7+T8 can start 789. So T5 and T7/T8 are useful.\n- **Keep T5, T7, T8** as future chow material.\n- **Keep B7\u00d72**: possible pair. If we win with B7 pair, adds structure. Also, only one B7 is out (in opponent\u2019s meld), so two remain in wall \u2014 decent chance.\n- **Keep J3**: supports Reversible Tiles (8 points), which requires only reversible tiles. Our current hand: W2(no), W3(no), T1(no), T5(yes), T7(no), T8(yes), B7(no), J3(yes). Too many non-reversible. Not viable.\n- **Best keepers**: W3\u00d73 (potential pung), T5/T7/T8 (for Mixed Straight), B7\u00d72 (pair), J3 (minor option).\n\nThus, worst tile to keep: **W2** \u2014 singleton, not supporting any strong pattern, and W1 is mostly gone (Peng by opponent), so no chow possible.\n\nBut wait \u2014 valid actions include **Play W3**, not Play W2 per se. Among valid *plays*, W3 is listed \u2014 but we have three! Playing W3 would discard one of the three, leaving two \u2014 still good for possible future pung.\n\nSo compare safest discard:\n\n- **Play W3**: Sacrifices one of three W3s. Loses some value toward concealed pung (2 pts). But if not committed to that, acceptable.\n- **Play T1**: Already used in a Chow. We have only one T1. Can\u2019t use again. Low utility. But T1 is terminal, not helpful for All Simples.\n- **Play T5/T7/T8**: All could support Mixed Straight. T5 is key for 456; T7+T8 can form 789. Should keep.\n- **Play J3**: Only one, and J3 is useful for Reversible or Melded Kong later. But not urgent. Safer to keep than discard.\n- **Play B7**: Have two. Discarding one leaves one \u2014 breaks potential pair. Riskier.\n\nTherefore, least valuable tile to keep: **T1** \u2014 already used in chi, terminal, doesn\u2019t contribute to All Simples or Mixed Straight beyond what\u2019s already built.\n\nWait \u2014 valid action list includes **Play T1**, yes.\n\nBut let's double-check: Do we even have T1 in hand? Hand: [W2:1; W3:3; T1:1; T5:1; T7:1; T8:1; B7:2; J3:1] \u2014 yes, we do. But we used T1 in the Chi [T1,T2,T3], which means we must have drawn T1 after forming the meld? Or did we use a discarded T1 to form the chow?\n\nAh \u2014 important: The action \"Chi T2\" appears in our history. Wait \u2014 \u201cChi Tile\u201d notation: 'Chi T2' means we formed a Chow using a discarded tile to complete [T1,T2,T3]. Since it was T2 discarded, we must have held T1 and T3 to complete it.\n\nBut now we still have T1 in hand? That suggests either:\n- We drew another T1 after forming the chow, or\n- The game state allows holding extra tiles post-meld.\n\nIn standard rules, after forming a chow, you keep those tiles in meld area, and maintain 13 live tiles. So having T1 now means we drew it back \u2014 possible.\n\nBut logically, if we already used T1 in a chow, why keep another? It doesn't help much.\n\nHowever, T1 is a terminal \u2014 bad for All Simples. And we already have a chow in T1-T3 \u2014 no benefit from second T1.\n\nCompare to W2: W2 is a simple tile, could be part of 234 chow \u2014 but we have no T2/T3/T4 except in other suits. Not helpful.\n\nBut W3\u00d73 is strong \u2014 better to keep those.\n\nAmong options, **T1** is weakest: terminal, not helping Mixed Straight (we already have 123 via meld), not helping All Simples.\n\nSimilarly, **T8** could help 789 chow. **T7+T8** together are promising.\n\n**J3** \u2014 honor, but reversible. Not great.\n\nBest discard candidates: **T1**, **W2**, **J3**\n\nBut W2 is not in valid actions? Wait \u2014 valid actions: [Play W3; Play T8; Play T5; Play T1; Play W2; Play T7; Play J3; Play B7]\n\nYes, **Play W2** is available.\n\nW2: we have only one. Cannot form chow without W1/W3 \u2014 W1 is mostly gone (3 in Peng by opponent), so only one left. Unlikely. And we have W3\u00d73 \u2014 could form 333, not 234.\n\nSo W2 is useless.\n\nLikewise, T1 is useless.\n\nBetween W2 and T1: both are weak.\n\nBut **T1 is terminal**, worse for hand purity. **W2 is simple**, slightly better.\n\nAlso, **W3\u00d73** \u2014 we should keep at least two. Playing one W3 is acceptable, but not ideal.\n\nBut among valid plays, **Play T1** removes a terminal tile, improving chance for **All Simples** (if we can get rid of honors and terminals).\n\nCurrent hand has: T1 (terminal), T8 (simple), T7 (simple), T5 (simple), W2 (simple), W3 (simple), B7 (simple), J3 (honor)\n\nTerminals: T1 only\n\nHonors: J3, and B7 is simple\n\nSo only two non-simples: T1 and J3\n\nIf we discard T1, then only J3 remains \u2014 then if we replace J3 later, we can achieve **All Simples** (2 points)\n\nAlternatively, discard J3 first.\n\nBut **J3** can contribute to **Reversible Tiles** (8 points) \u2014 which requires only reversible tiles.\n\nList of reversible tiles: B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B8,B9, T2,T4,T5,T6,T8,T9, J3\n\nOur hand:\n- W2: no \u2192 invalid\n- W3: no \u2192 invalid\n- T1: no\n- T5: yes\n- T7: no (T7 not symmetric)\n- T8: yes\n- B7: no\n- J3: yes\n\nToo many non-reversible. Cannot achieve Reversible Tiles.\n\nSo J3 only value is as honor pung (but we don\u2019t have three), or in Outside Hand.\n\nBetter to **discard T1** \u2014 it\u2019s a terminal, hurts All Simples, and doesn\u2019t help any major pattern.\n\nAlternatively, **discard J3** \u2014 same logic.\n\nBut **T1** is worse because:\n- Terminal + suit\n- Doesn\u2019t help Mixed Straight (we already have 123 meld)\n\nWhereas T5, T7, T8 are central to building 456 and 789 for **Mixed Straight** (8 points)\n\nWe have T5, T7, T8 \u2014 almost ready to form 456 (need T4/T6) and 789 (need T9)\n\nSo keep them.\n\nB7\u00d72: could be winning pair. Only one B7 used in opponent\u2019s meld \u2014 two left. Decent shot.\n\nW3\u00d73: concealed pung = 2 points. Keep.\n\nSo worst tiles: T1, J3, W2\n\nAmong valid plays, **Play T1** removes a terminal cleanly.\n\n**Play J3** removes an honor.\n\nBoth help **All Simples**.\n\nBut T1 is less reusable \u2014 we already have a T-chow.\n\nJ3 might be held for future kong opportunity? But no sign.\n\nAlso, **F2 and J2** are both punged by next player \u2014 so likely safe to discard J3.\n\nBut J3 has 4 unseen \u2014 very safe.\n\nT1: 2 unseen \u2014 also safe.\n\nBut **T1 is more harmful to pure hands**.\n\nFinal decision: **Discard T1** to improve hand toward All Simples and eliminate a terminal.\n\nReason: T1 is a terminal tile that does not contribute to any high-scoring pattern. We already have a Chow in 1-2-3 Bamboos, making a second T1 redundant. Removing it improves the chance of achieving \"All Simples\" (2 points) and streamlines the hand. Other tiles like T5, T7, T8 are crucial for building toward \"Mixed Straight\" (8 points), W3\u00d73 offers a concealed pung (2 points), and B7\u00d72 can serve as a winning pair. J3 is neutral but less damaging than a terminal.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
